[ChapterName "Blackburne Schilling Gambit"]
[UTCDate "2016.06.01"]
[UTCTime "16:11:04"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 { The Blackburne Schilling Gambit begins with C50, Italian Game. } 3... Nd4 { Continues with the unusual black play, Nd4. } 4. Nxe5 { White's greedy downfall begins with the seemingly innocent Nxe5. } 4... Qg5 5. Nxf7 { It would seem that white's move is excellent, but black will prove him wrong. } (5. Bxf7+ { White can potentially recover by checking with bishop.
William Steinitz has this to say about white's position that "followed by castling, [this] is now White's best chance and in some measure a promising one, considering that he has two Pawns and the attack for the piece". } 5... Ke7 6. O-O Qxe5 7. Bc4 { Retreat and recollect. } (7. Bxg8 { Or trade for knight. Either way, white will have lost the knight for his greedy grab for the e-pawn. } 7... Rxg8 8. c3 Nc6 9. d4 Qf6 { Jeremy Silman finds White accommodated for his lost knight in this position. })) 5... Qxg2 6. Rf1 { White realizes his error now, and attempts to recover. } 6... Qxe4+ 7. Be2 { One of white's two choices while in check and undoubtedly the worse. } (7. Qe2 Nxe2 { And yet, the second option is no better. Resignation impending. }) 7... Nf3# { A classic, ideal smothered mate. } *


