{ The Légal Trap is named after Sire de Légal. The trap was used effectively by British master Joseph Henry Blackburne. }
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 { Starts with C50, Italian Game } 3... d6 4. Nc3 Bg4 { Black "pins" the knight to the queen. } 5. h3 { This begins white committal to the trap. } (5. Nxe5 { If white attempted the trap at this point, the black knight can capture the white knight and protect the bishop from the queen. By playing h3, the black bishop has to move to an unprotected square. } 5... Nxe5 (5... Bxd1) 6. Qxg4 Nxg4) 5... Bh5 6. Nxe5 { Here white as offered up his queen. } 6... Bxd1 { If black takes the bait, it's mate in two. } (6... Nxe5 { If black notices the trap and captures the knight... } 7. Qxh5 { White can equalize with the black bishop. } 7... Nxc4 { And further equalize with the fork on king and knight. } 8. Qb5+ c6 9. Qxc4) (6... dxe5 7. Qxh5) 7. Bxf7+ Ke7 8. Nd5# *


