1. d4 d5 2. e4 dxe4 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. f3 exf3 { Starts with D00, Blackmar-Diemar Gambit } 5. Qxf3 (5. Nxf3 { This seems to be the most common continuation; another sound opening. }) 5... Qxd4 { Black thinks he just got a free pawn. } 6. Be3 Qb4 { ...threatening Qxb2. } 7. O-O-O Bg4 { Black thinks he is clever to pin the queen to the rook. } 8. Nb5 { At this move, black's prospects worsen, for there are many pitfalls. } 8... Bxf3 (8... Na6 { Black can protect the square. } 9. Qxb7 { But then the queen can come in and threaten. } 9... Bxd1 (9... Qe4 { Here is probably the best counter to white. } 10. Qxa6 { However, the queen will continue pillaging. } 10... Qxe3+ 11. Kb1) 10. Nxc7+ Nxc7 11. Qxb4 { Oops. Queen left unguarded. }) 9. Nxc7# { Checkmate. Enjoy the queen, white'll enjoy the king. } *


